<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.Notifications</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.Notifications</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Notifications &amp; Communication Platform (Phase B13). The platform's single owner of how a
      fact reaches a person.

      This is not a notification table. It is the orchestration layer between "something happened"
      and "somebody was told", and the distinction is the whole design: every other module publishes
      facts and knows nothing about messages, while this module knows nothing about the facts and
      everything about people, preferences, channels, languages and timing.

      What Communication owns: the notification itself, the member's inbox and its counters, the
      preference and communication-preference record, the template catalogue and its localizations,
      the delivery record per channel, digests, the notification category catalogue, the routing
      rules, and the analytics rollups. What it deliberately does not own: any business fact. There
      is no job, listing, event or guide in this schema — only an identifier, a template key and a
      deep link. The device register belongs to Identity and is reached through a port; the
      photograph belongs to Media; the verification belongs to Trust; the destination behind a deep
      link belongs to whichever module renders it.

      Business modules never create notifications. They publish integration events, and this module
      consumes them. A rule — reference data, not code — decides which template, which category,
      which priority and which delivery policy each published fact produces, so changing "an
      interview reminder is Critical and bypasses quiet hours" is a catalogue change rather than a
      deployment.

      In-app delivery is fully implemented. Push and email are implemented as channels behind
      provider-independent ports (IPushNotificationSender, IEmailSender) whose only shipped
      implementations record the attempt and report that no transport is configured — so the
      lifecycle, the retry ladder, the analytics and the preference logic are all exercised today
      and acquiring a vendor is a registration swap. SMS, WhatsApp, voice and webhooks are channel
      kinds with no implementation, present so adding one is a new class rather than a new concept.

      Marketing campaigns, real-time chat, AI-generated content, AI prioritisation, recommendation,
      calendar synchronisation and a search engine implementation are explicitly out of scope; each
      has an abstraction shaped for it and no code behind it.

      Registers entirely through B2.6 contributor interfaces: no edit to the Platform Kernel, to any
      previously completed module, or to Program.cs.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately the narrowest reference set of any vertical in the platform, and that is the point.
    Communication consumes published contracts and nothing else, so it references Diaspora.Contracts
    for the events it subscribes to and takes no ProjectReference on Trust, Community, Guides, Jobs,
    Marketplace, Housing, Community Life, Providers or Location at all. A notification about a job
    interview is assembled entirely from the payload of JobsInterviewScheduledIntegrationEvent; if
    that payload is not enough, the fix is to enrich the contract, never to reach into the module.

    Diaspora.Application is referenced for the CQRS abstractions and the integration-event handler
    base; Diaspora.Persistence for IDatabaseContributor and the shared context. Architecture tests
    enforce the absence of every module reference.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.SharedKernel\Diaspora.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Contracts\Diaspora.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Platform\Diaspora.Platform.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Application\Diaspora.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Persistence\Diaspora.Persistence.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
